    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50001,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49001,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "You are met with unequivocal rejection from your progressive peers, who remember your treatment of Franklin Roosevelt when he began running for president. Their emboldened bloc at the convention eventually scores a victory over the southern delegates, officially nominating Henry Wallace for president, with Glen Taylor thrown in as well, signifying a true continuation of Franklin Roosevelt and his legacy."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50002,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49002,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "“Well,” Russell says, his drawl extending the word much longer than necessary, “I do see you as a potential candidate for the Vice Presidency, but for right now we have plenty of great candidates who may be nominated by us to take down that commie Wallace.” You hang your head slightly and sigh as you go back to the convention floor."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50003,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49003,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "The two mayors take a liking to you; they always have. They, also fearful of a Wallace nomination, begin working their states’ delegations, penciling in your name as a potential compromise candidate should their man be lost in translation come balloting time. However, many in the urban camp are still very skeptical of you, wondering if you are still the same Paul McNutt who sent the National Guard to quell strikes."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50004,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49004,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "With your support now behind the Wallace wing of the party, their numbers tick just a little further up, but it is still not enough to secure the nomination. Wallace fumes in his hotel room that night, as those stubborn bastards down South will not give an inch to him. You privately tell him that you still seek the presidency, since your alliance no longer works toward a majority. He understands."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50005,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49005,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Despite your support being thrown to Russell, his support is stalled quickly by the progressives of the party, standing in unison to block Russell’s segregationist agenda from ever making it to the stage of the convention hall. Galvanized by a speech from a young Senate candidate from up north, the progressives refuse to budge, leaving the convention nearly in the same spot it started from."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50006,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49006,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "You are able to scoop up a few delegates, but your numbers remain paltry in comparison to both Wallace and Russell. A few more ballots pass, with both candidates getting only slightly closer to victory. An anxious public awaits the results, with many Democrats wondering if Roosevelt was the only one able to bring them together, recalling the party’s chaotic conventions of 1920 and 1924."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50007,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49007,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Your tepid rejection of this plank has caused the Anyone but Wallace caucus to nominate you as a sort of compromise between him and Russell, viewing you as a candidate who won’t ruffle any feathers besides those most fiercely in the camp of Wallace, who do not have enough of a following to walk out anyhow. You are nominated on the 7th ballot, with a few too many onlookers with indifferent expressions on their faces."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50008,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49008,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Historians will just remember you as the man who gave someone else the nomination. Your stance finally gives the progressives the second wind they need, propelling them to the nomination. The Southerners are able to make sure it isn’t someone as radical as Wallace, but Hubert Humphrey is able to ascend much quicker than he ever expected. Southerners groan, but there isn’t anything they can do about it."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50009,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49009,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "The party bosses get moving. Immediately, both sides are quelled easily, each one of them going into the smoke-filled rooms to decide the next candidate. You, of course, are one of their first choices, and you see little opposition from anyone as you amble up to the stage in front of thousands of screaming delegates. It's amazing how far you can get just based on who you know."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50010,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49010,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "The convention floor erupts in applause as you step down from your podium, shaking many people’s hands along the way. However, while Roosevelt and what he was able to accomplish are still very popular, there are still a great many who are skeptical of the former president, especially after his hiding of his various illnesses that plagued his final term in office."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50011,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49011,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "You sound quite a bit like the President himself when you say this, and it plays well enough. Youth is not an attack that has been used too much on a presidential candidate, and it does feel as if Dewey may have jumped the gun on the presidency a few years too early with him being the youngest president ever inaugurated."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50012,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49012,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Dewey has not been the New Deal shredder many thought he would be in office, but he certainly hasn’t been kind to the programs either. Emphasizing your party as the party of those programs helps you slightly in the Midwest, the states where many people are bitten the hardest by Dewey’s cold and calculated approach to the vast achievements of Franklin Roosevelt’s 12 years in office."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50013,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49013,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Progressives cheer wildly at this speech, and many were not expecting this angle from you. Franklin Roosevelt was a great man, but his wealth oftentimes blinded him from the true problems that the country faced, as Hoover’s had for much of his adult life. You have always been a politician, a man of the people. That is what the people want, more than anything."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50014,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49014,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Maybank seems to offend almost nobody. He’s enough to give the southern delegates a reason to vote for you in November, and is not racist enough to make progressives become aghast at your campaign. He is a happy medium, all things considered."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50015,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49015,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Reuther looks at you condescendingly as you appear next to him in a photo for the papers. He’s never liked you, and when you get him alone with you, he has nothing but bad memories of you and your governorship. He tells you he has no plans of getting the vote out for you in November, despite your recent overtures to him and his union empire he has built across the Midwest."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50016,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49016,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Uncle Dick can’t make it, as he has more pressing matters to attend to. You are instead met with Senator John Sparkman, one of the newer faces in the upper chamber, alongside another relative newcomer in James Eastland, a much more violently southern counterpart to Sparkman. They discuss matters on protecting the South’s way of life. You tell them you will not lay a finger on any of it. Slowly, the machines in the South creak back to life, suppressing any attempt at an unpledged campaign."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50017,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49017,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Attlee likes you, that much is clear, but your time away from the campaign trail, paired with Dewey’s unmatched foreign policy skills in comparison to your own, makes the entire trip fall flat on its face. You hastily return to America, but the damage is already done. Your friends in Indiana shake their heads, wondering why you would do a thing so brash."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50018,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49018,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Welles’ booming voice commands the airwaves just like it does in all of his movies, captivating audiences. Your tour of Warner Brothers Studios is also well-publicized, your good looks playing well for the media hungry for a candidate they can hound since the President doesn’t seem to be the kindest towards reporters."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50019,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49019,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Playing the Hoover card has lost some of the luster it once carried when Roosevelt was running for president, but it still works better than most every attack you have lobbed at Dewey thus far. For once, your lukewarm relations with labor are thrown to the side in favor of some nice photo ops at factories littered across the Midwest."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50020,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49020,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Independents cheer at your words, and you get a few endorsements from moderate newspapers around the West Coast. The East, however, still stands behind their president, committed to doing whatever they can in support of Dewey. Some party chairs, however, scold you at your decision to praise anyone from the opposing party."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50021,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49021,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Taft-Hartley is an issue many people have forgotten about, with all of the hoopla around many of Dewey’s most sparkling foreign policy achievements. They had forgotten just how much they hated the act and begin to attack the President for it vigorously, leaving him with yet another headache to worry about."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50022,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49022,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "This is a broadly beneficial way of orienting your view. The HUAC’s popularity is declining sharply, and tying Dewey to its growth is a solid maneuver, one that will surely have the public questioning whether or not its dear president is as much of a people’s president as he claims to be."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50023,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49023,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Slowly, “Veterans for McNutt” chapters are sprinkled across the country, with their home base in Indianapolis. You get 50,000 signees within the first week, each one of them opposed to communism’s spread with an intensity only rivaled by Harold Stassen in the Dewey administration. You get a small check in the mail from one of his family members, a “donation to save democracy.”"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50024,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49024,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Attacking the Republican Party at large is smart. Many voters might not even know what the HUAC even is, so it is the better move to attack something that they are more acquainted with. Richard Nixon is among the least popular representatives in the country, having barely been elected two years ago. They wish he would focus on the real things. He never will."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50025,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49025,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "It is not exactly in Speaker Rayburn’s authority to do so, but he begins stripping the organization back in subtle ways. He keeps committee assignments away, gets bills against it to the floor, and chokes funding toward the HUAC. The fanfare of the organization dies out quickly, giving you and the party a much-needed win."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50026,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49026,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Nuclear weaponry is an invention that even its creators regret, so going against its usage should be a no-brainer, but actually, it is not that simple. The public, though against the end of civilization, mostly has no problem with nuclear development, as a way to build up our defenses against the Soviets, who could pose a very serious threat as they develop their own weapons, no doubt stolen from our own plans."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50027,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49027,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "This is the majority opinion right now. Nuclear energy is currently capturing the hearts and minds of the public, and taking away their delusions of the future would be a bad thing. Since Dewey is taking a pragmatic approach, it is hard to win on this issue, but this is about as close as you are going to get."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50028,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49028,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "A few bitter, diehard leftists peel off from your support after hearing this from you, but many just brush off your statement and move on. You receive a letter from Director of Los Alamos Norris Bradbury, who implores your action on the issue. You ignore it, and move on to more pressing matters. You still have a long way to go before those union men will ever trust you."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50029,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49029,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "The air is cloudy and filled with smoke as you speak in front of the very same enameling plant that you sent the military to. Many of the same workers are still there 13 years later, working the exact same job and likely still for very little pay. They do not see you as a friend. Small tremors are felt in the city a few days later. A local newspaper reports, “McNutt sends Eugene Debs rolling.”"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50030,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49030,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Some of them do, begrudgingly, see the light. Some local unions across your home state endorse you, alongside a few national ones. However, many are still apprehensive about voting for anyone in this election. Pollsters report that union turnout will be close to 25%, a shockingly low number for a group so politically charged. Most blame you."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50031,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49031,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "You send intermediaries to talk to Wallace, and after a while, he agrees, but not with a smile. In his home state of Iowa, he speaks in front of a crowd of farmers, telling them that you are the only thing standing between success and failure for them. Des Moines meets you a bit more warmly the next time you go. You thank your lucky stars."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50032,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49032,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "The many Slavic groups in America look toward your campaign in silent admiration, but this does not seem to be a very effective message. The people believe Dewey is doing what he can in such a crisis, and your accusations that he is not doing enough seem distasteful. They would rather have half of the children fed than none at all."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50033,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49033,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "An odd way to attack Dewey, but it pays off dramatically. You are able to weaken Dewey’s tough-on-communism approach by making it seem like he isn’t doing enough to stop the Red Menace. Many of the most vigorous anti-communists in Congress begin criticizing Dewey on the Airlift, citing “ignorance of the issue of Communism in our country.”"
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50034,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49034,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Your story plays quite well with the public, especially within Dewey’s home state of New York, where many of the Jewish faith are grateful to you for your support. You come across one in Manhattan whose cousin’s life was saved by you and your efforts to get the Jews out of Germany. You’ve gained the upper hand on Dewey."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50035,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49035,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Two in three Americans attend some sort of film screening every week. You have the ad run during intermission, while most people are refilling their popcorn and getting themselves more Coca-Cola. The people who do see it aren’t impressed, viewing your ad as a cheap rip-off of what Dewey ran just a couple of weeks earlier. On paper, it is a net zero, but you spent quite a bit on the ad."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50036,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49036,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Mrs. Roosevelt, not as distraught over her husband’s death as you might have thought, agrees wholeheartedly. Though she has had her reservations with you, especially considering Franklin’s distaste for you, she puts aside her personal issues and gets on the radio for you. It's great nostalgia for many Americans who remember her late husband’s fireside chats."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50037,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49037,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Your dialect is not too dissimilar from your opponent’s, but your passionate and authoritative voice carries across much better than Dewey’s didactic lawyer tone. You get plenty of positive press from national papers, talking about how you are the man who will bring the Democratic Party out of its sudden brush with darkness."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50038,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49038,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "It's chilly out in St. Louis, Missouri. In front of the mighty Mississippi, you give an amazing speech to the crowd, your voice energizing the hundreds in attendance. If there were still lingering doubts about your loyalty to the party, given your past endeavors against Franklin Roosevelt, there certainly aren’t anymore now."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50039,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49039,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Progressives applaud you for this stance, with even Glen Taylor giving much praise to your proposed tax plan. However, Dewey’s campaign is quick to respond, with a few sharp jabs at you for “discouraging domestic and international investment.” They want businesses to succeed, but you cannot possibly fathom putting businesses before people. It’s not like businesses are also people."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50040,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49040,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "You are able to assuage many of the Democrats who got you the nomination a short time ago, but a sizable faction in the party stirs at your words. Organized labor, your Achilles heel this campaign, calls you out on this for failing to substantially differentiate your tax plan from the President’s. Dewey has made mention of you agreeing with him on a lot, and this stance does not help you."
        }
    },
    {
        "model": "campaign_trail.answer_feedback",
        "pk": 50041,
        "fields": {
            "answer": 49041,
            "candidate": 116,
            "answer_feedback": "Such a fiery denunciation of Dewey’s policy shocks even the most ardent laborites, who now seem to move with a different opinion towards you. To most everyone else, however, this is seen as something a little too progressive for their tastes. Taxes are already extremely high on the wealthiest earners, so why increase them even more? It doesn’t make sense to most."
        }
